<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Jimara (Kaabu)</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three royal provinces of the Kaabu Empire, stretching from Guinea-Bissau toward the Gambia river.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jimara was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Mandinka Kaabu Empire</a>, which emerged from the westward expansion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and became independent in the 16th century. It lay in the north-east of Kaabu, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> river. Its history is known mainly from oral tradition.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With Sama and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pachana-kaabu">Pachana</a>, Jimara was one of the provinces whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyantio">Nyancho</a> warrior nobility, reckoned through the female line, supplied the Mansaba of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a> in rotation. Its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a> ruled with local lineage heads, and its economy rested on farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> and trade with river ports.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Jimara was weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> rebellions and attacks from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Imamate of Futa Jallon</a> in the 19th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu's</a> fall in 1867 its lands were absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fuladu">Fuladu</a> and later divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">British Gambia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.30%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1867 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mandinka traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandinka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, <a href="/countries/gambia">Gambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jimara, Djimara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=jimara-kaabu" aria-label="Jimara (Kaabu) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4281 1724 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jimara (Kaabu) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4560 1941h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4601 1980h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4601 1980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Guinea-Bissau. <a href="/atlas?year=1684&amp;focus=jimara-kaabu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

